    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d446">

                <pb facs="0790" n="658" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv01/pg658"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.00/7–745: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 446<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-07-07T00:00:00+03:00">July 7,
                            1945—midnight</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2472. Sharp protest was made by Brit Ambassador<anchor n="1" xml:id="d446-f1"/>
                    in letter to Molotov dated July 6 (to
                        Dept 2472 rptd Athens 31, London 348) regarding recent criticism in Soviet
                    Press and radio of Greek Govt, Brit policy in Greece and conduct of Scobie and Brit troops in that country.
                    Statement in Shalitov article in <hi rendition="#italic">New Times</hi> June 15
                    on Greece described by Clark Kerr as misleading and hostile.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
